\mt1 Romans
\c 1
\s1 Paul Greets the Saints in Rome
\p \v 1 Paul, a servant of Christ Jesus, called \add to be an\add* apostle, \add and\add* set apart for \add the\add* gospel of God— \v 2 \add the gospel\add* He promised beforehand through His prophets in \add the\add* Holy Scriptures, \v 3 regarding His Son, who was a descendant of David according to \add the\add* flesh, \v 4 \add and\add* who through \add the\add* Spirit of holiness was declared with power \add to be the\add* Son of God by \add His\add* resurrection \add from the\add* dead: Jesus Christ our Lord.
\p \v 5 Through \add Him and\add* on behalf of His name, we received grace and apostleship to call all \add those\add* among the Gentiles \add to the\add* obedience that comes from faith. \v 6 \add And\add* you also are among those who are called \add to belong to\add* Jesus Christ.
\p \v 7 To all in Rome who are loved by God \add and\add* called to be saints:
\p Grace and peace to you from God our Father and \add the\add* Lord Jesus Christ.
\s1 Unashamed of the Gospel
\p \v 8 First, I thank my God through Jesus Christ for all of you, because your faith is being proclaimed all over the world. \v 9 God, whom I serve with my spirit\f + \fr 1:9 \ft Or \fqa in my spirit\f* in \add preaching\add* the gospel of His Son, is my witness how constantly I remember you \v 10 in my prayers at all times, asking that now at last by God’s will I may succeed in coming to you. \v 11 For I long to see you so that I may impart to you some spiritual gift to strengthen you, \v 12 that is, \add that\add* you and I may be mutually encouraged by each other’s faith.
\p \v 13 I do not want you to be unaware, brothers, how often I planned to come to you (\add but\add* have been prevented \add from visiting\add*\f + \fr 1:13 \ft Literally \fqa but have been prevented\f* until now), in order that I might have a harvest among you, just as \add I have had\add* among the other Gentiles. \v 14 I am obligated both to Greeks and non-Greeks,\f + \fr 1:14 \ft Literally \fqa to Greeks and barbarians\f* both to \add the\add* wise and \add the\add* foolish. \v 15 That is why I am \add so\add* eager to preach the gospel also to you who \add are\add* in Rome.
\p \v 16 I am not ashamed of the gospel, because it is \add the\add* power of God for salvation to everyone who believes, first to \add the\add* Jew, then to \add the\add* Greek. \v 17 For \add the gospel\add* reveals \add the\add* righteousness of God \add that comes\add* by faith \add from start\add* to \add finish\add*,\f + \fr 1:17 \ft Literally \fqa For in it is revealed the righteousness of God from faith to faith\f* just as it is written: “The righteous will live by faith.”\f + \fr 1:17 \ref Habakkuk 2:4|HAB 2:4\ref*\f*
\s1 God’s Wrath against Sin
\p \v 18 \add The\add* wrath of God is being revealed from heaven against all \add the\add* godlessness and wickedness of men who suppress the truth by \add their\add* wickedness. \v 19 For \add what may be\add* known \add about\add* God is plain to them, because God has made \add it\add* plain to them. \v 20 For since \add the\add* creation of \add the\add* world \add God’s\add* invisible qualities, His eternal power and divine nature, have been clearly seen, being understood from His workmanship, so that \add men\add* are without excuse.
\p \v 21 For although they knew God, they neither glorified \add Him\add* as God \add nor\add* gave thanks \add to Him\add*, but they became futile in their thinking and darkened in their foolish hearts. \v 22 Although they claimed to be wise, they became fools, \v 23 and exchanged the glory of the immortal God for images of mortal man and birds and animals and reptiles.
\p \v 24 Therefore God gave them over in the desires of their hearts to impurity for the dishonoring of their bodies with \add one another\add*. \v 25 \add They\add* exchanged the truth of God for a lie, and worshiped and served the creature rather than the Creator, who is forever worthy of praise!\f + \fr 1:25 \ft Or \fqa forever blessed\f* Amen.
\p \v 26 For this reason God gave them over to dishonorable passions. Even their women exchanged natural relations for unnatural \add ones\add*. \v 27 Likewise, the men abandoned natural relations with women \add and\add* burned with lust for one another. Men committed indecent \add acts\add* with \add other\add* men and received in themselves the due penalty for their error.
\p \v 28 Furthermore, since they did not see fit to acknowledge God, \add He\add* gave them up to a depraved mind, to do what ought not to be done. \v 29 They have become filled with every kind of wickedness, evil, greed, \add and\add* depravity. \add They are\add* full of envy, murder, strife, deceit, \add and\add* malice. \add They are\add* gossips, \v 30 slanderers, God-haters, insolent, arrogant, \add and\add* boastful. \add They\add* invent \add new\add* forms of evil; they disobey \add their\add* parents. \v 31 \add They are\add* senseless, faithless, heartless, merciless.
\p \v 32 \add Although they\add* know God’s righteous decree that those who do such things are worthy of death, \add they\add* not only continue to do these things, but also approve of those who practice \add them\add*.
\c 2
\s1 God’s Righteous Judgment
\r (\ref Psalm 75:1–10|PSA 75:1-10\ref*)
\p \v 1 You, therefore, have no excuse, \add you\add* who pass judgment \add on another\add*. For on whatever grounds you judge the other, you are condemning yourself, because \add you who\add* pass judgment do the same things. \v 2 And we know that God’s judgment against those who do such things is based on truth. \v 3 \add So when you\add*, O man, pass judgment on \add others\add*, yet do the same things, do you think you will escape God’s judgment? \v 4 Or do you disregard the riches of His kindness, tolerance, and patience, not realizing that God’s kindness leads you to repentance?
\p \v 5 But because of your hard and unrepentant heart, you are storing up wrath against yourself for \add the\add* day of wrath, \add when\add* God’s righteous judgment will be revealed. \v 6 \add God\add* “will repay each one according to his deeds.”\f + \fr 2:6 \ref Psalm 62:12|PSA 62:12\ref*\f* \v 7 To those who by perseverance in doing good seek glory, honor, and immortality, \add He will give\add* eternal life. \v 8 But for those who \add are\add* self-seeking and who reject the truth and follow wickedness, \add there will be\add* wrath and anger.
\p \v 9 \add There will be\add* trouble and distress for every human being who does evil, first for the Jew, then for the Greek; \v 10 but glory, honor, and peace for everyone who does good, first for the Jew, then for the Greek. \v 11 For God does not show favoritism.
\p \v 12 All who sin apart from the law will also perish apart from the law, and all who sin under \add the\add* law will be judged by \add the\add* law. \v 13 For \add it is\add* not the hearers of \add the\add* law \add who are\add* righteous before God, but \add it is\add* the doers of \add the\add* law \add who\add* will be declared righteous.
\p \v 14 Indeed, when Gentiles, who do not have \add the\add* law, do by nature what the law \add requires\add*, they are a law to themselves, \add even though\add* they do not have \add the\add* law. \v 15 \add So\add* they show \add that\add* the work of the law is written on their hearts, their consciences also bearing witness, \add and\add* their thoughts either accusing or defending \add them\add* \v 16 on the day when God will judge men’s secrets through Christ Jesus,\f + \fr 2:16 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa through Jesus Christ\f* as proclaimed by my gospel.
\s1 The Jews and the Law
\p \v 17 Now you, if you call \add yourself\add* a Jew; \add if you\add* rely on \add the\add* law and boast in God; \v 18 \add if you\add* know \add His\add* will and approve of what is superior because you are instructed by the law; \v 19 \add if you\add* are convinced \add that\add* you are a guide for \add the\add* blind, a light for those in darkness, \v 20 an instructor of \add the\add* foolish, a teacher of infants, \add because\add* you have in the law the embodiment of knowledge and truth — \v 21 \add you,\add* then, who teach others, do you not teach yourself? You who preach against stealing, do you steal? \v 22 You who forbid adultery, do you commit adultery? You who abhor idols, do you rob temples? \v 23 You who boast in \add the\add* law, do you dishonor God by breaking the law? \v 24 As it is written: “God’s name is blasphemed among the Gentiles because of you.”\f + \fr 2:24 \ref Isaiah 52:5|ISA 52:5\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 25 Circumcision has value if you observe \add the\add* law, but if you break \add the\add* law, your circumcision has become uncircumcision. \v 26 If a man who is not circumcised keeps the requirements of the law, \add will\add* not his uncircumcision be regarded as circumcision? \v 27 The \add one who is\add* physically uncircumcised \add yet\add* keeps the law will condemn you who, \add even though you have the\add* written code and circumcision, \add are\add* a lawbreaker.
\p \v 28 A \add man\add* is not a Jew \add because he is one \add* outwardly, nor \add is\add* circumcision \add only \add* outward \add and\add* physical. \v 29 No, a man \add is\add* a Jew \add because he is one \add* inwardly, and circumcision \add is a matter\add* of the heart, by \add the\add* Spirit, not by the written code. \add Such a man’s\add* praise \add does\add* not \add come\add* from men, but from God.
\c 3
\s1 God Remains Faithful
\p \v 1 What, then, \add is\add* the advantage of \add being\add* a Jew? Or what \add is\add* the value of circumcision? \v 2 Much in every way. First of all, they have been entrusted with the very words\f + \fr 3:2 \ft Or \fqa the oracles\f* of God.
\p \v 3 What if some did not have faith? \add Will\add* their lack of faith nullify God’s faithfulness? \v 4 Certainly not! \add Let\add* God be true and every man a liar. As it is written:
\b 
\q1 “So that You may be proved right when You speak
\q2 and victorious when You judge.”\f + \fr 3:4 \ft Or \fqa when You are judged\ft ; \ref Psalm 51:4|PSA 51:4\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 5 But if our unrighteousness highlights the righteousness of God, what shall we say? \add That\add* God \add is\add* unjust to inflict \add His\add* wrath \add on us\add*? I am speaking in human terms. \v 6 Certainly not! In that case, how \add could\add* God judge the world? \v 7 However, if my falsehood accentuates God’s truthfulness, to the increase of His glory, why \add am\add* I still condemned as a sinner? \v 8 \add Why\add* not \add say\add*, as some slanderously claim \add that\add* we say, “Let us do evil that good may result”? Their condemnation is deserved!
\s1 There Is No One Righteous
\r (\ref Psalm 14:1–7|PSA 14:1-7\ref*; \ref Psalm 53:1–6|PSA 53:1-6\ref*; \ref Isaiah 59:1–17|ISA 59:1-17\ref*)
\p \v 9 What then? Are we any better? Not at all. For we have already made the charge \add that\add* Jews and Greeks alike are all under sin. \v 10 As it is written:
\b 
\q1 “There is no one righteous,
\q2 not even one.
\q1 \v 11 There is no one who understands,
\q2 no one who seeks God.
\q1 \v 12 All have turned away,
\q2 they have together become worthless;
\q1 there is no one who does good,
\q2 not even one.”\f + \fr 3:12 \ref Psalm 14:1–3|PSA 14:1-3\ref* and Psalm 53:1–3 (see also LXX)\f*
\q1 \v 13 “Their throats \add are\add* open graves;
\q2 their tongues practice deceit.”\f + \fr 3:13 \ref Psalm 5:9|PSA 5:9\ref*\f*
\q2 “\add The\add* venom of vipers \add is\add* on their lips.”\f + \fr 3:13 \ref Psalm 140:3|PSA 140:3\ref*\f*
\q1 \v 14 “\add Their\add* mouths are full
\q2 of cursing and bitterness.”\f + \fr 3:14 \ref Psalm 10:7|PSA 10:7\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\q1 \v 15 “Their feet \add are\add* swift to shed blood;
\q2 \v 16 ruin and misery \add lie\add* in their wake,
\q2 \v 17 and \add the\add* way of peace they have not known.”\f + \fr 3:17 \ref Isaiah 59:7–8|ISA 59:7-8\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\q1 \v 18 “There is no fear of God
\q2 before their eyes.”\f + \fr 3:18 \ref Psalm 36:1|PSA 36:1\ref*\f*
\p \v 19 Now we know that whatever the law says, it says to those who are under the law, so that every mouth may be silenced and the whole world held accountable to God. \v 20 Therefore no one will be justified in His sight by works of the law. For \add the\add* law merely brings awareness of sin.
\s1 Righteousness through Faith in Christ
\r (\ref Philippians 3:1–11|PHP 3:1-11\ref*)
\p \v 21 But now, apart from \add the\add* law, \add the\add* righteousness of God has been revealed, as attested by the Law and the Prophets. \v 22 And \add this\add* righteousness \add from\add* God \add comes\add* through faith in Jesus Christ to all who believe. There is no distinction, \v 23 for all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God, \v 24 \add and are\add* justified freely by His grace through the redemption that \add is\add* in Christ Jesus.
\p \v 25 God presented \add Him as an\add* atoning sacrifice\f + \fr 3:25 \ft Or \fqa as a propitiation\f* in His blood through faith, in order to demonstrate His righteousness, because in \add His\add* forbearance \add He\add* had passed over the sins committed beforehand. \v 26 \add He did this\add* to demonstrate His righteousness at the present time, so as to be just and to justify the \add one who has\add* faith in Jesus.
\p \v 27 Where, then, \add is\add* boasting? It is excluded. On what principle? \add On\add* that of works? No, but on \add that\add* of faith. \v 28 For we maintain that a man is justified by faith apart from works of the law.
\p \v 29 \add Is God\add* the God of Jews only? \add Is He\add* not \add the God\add* of Gentiles too? Yes, of Gentiles too, \v 30 since \add there is only\add* one God, who will justify \add the\add* circumcised by faith and \add the\add* uncircumcised through that \add same\add* faith.
\p \v 31 Do we, then, nullify \add the\add* law by \add this\add* faith? Certainly not! Instead, we uphold \add the\add* law.
\c 4
\s1 Abraham Justified by Faith
\r (\ref Genesis 15:1–7|GEN 15:1-7\ref*; \ref Psalm 32:1–11|PSA 32:1-11\ref*; \ref Hebrews 11:8–19|HEB 11:8-19\ref*)
\p \v 1 What then shall we say \add that\add* Abraham, our forefather according to \add the\add* flesh, has discovered? \v 2 If Abraham was indeed justified by works, he had something to boast about, but not before God. \v 3 For what \add does\add* the Scripture say? “Abraham believed God, and it was credited to him as righteousness.”\f + \fr 4:3 \ref Genesis 15:6|GEN 15:6\ref*\f*
\p \v 4 Now the wages of \add the\add* worker are not credited as a gift, but as an obligation. \v 5 However, to the \add one who\add* does not work, but believes in Him who justifies the ungodly, his faith is credited as righteousness. \v 6 And David speaks likewise of the blessedness of the man to whom God credits righteousness apart from works:
\b 
\q1 \v 7 “Blessed \add are they\add* whose lawless acts are forgiven,
\q2 whose sins are covered.
\q1 \v 8 Blessed \add is the\add* man
\q2 \add whose\add* sin \add the\add* Lord will never count against him.”\f + \fr 4:8 \ref Psalm 32:1–2|PSA 32:1-2\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 9 \add Is\add* this blessing \add only\add* on the circumcised, or also on the uncircumcised? We have been saying that Abraham’s faith was credited \add to him\add* as righteousness. \v 10 In what context was it credited? \add Was it after\add* his circumcision, or \add before\add*? \add It was\add* not \add after\add*, but \add before\add*.\f + \fr 4:10 \ft Literally \fqa How then was it reckoned—being in circumcision, or in uncircumcision? Not in circumcision, but in uncircumcision.\f*
\p \v 11 And he received \add the\add* sign of circumcision \add as\add* a seal of the righteousness that \add he had\add* by faith \add while he was still\add* uncircumcised. So then, he is \add the\add* father of all who believe \add but\add* are not circumcised, in order that righteousness might be credited to them. \v 12 And \add he is also the\add* father of the circumcised who not only are circumcised, but who also walk in the footsteps of the faith \add that\add* our father Abraham had before he was circumcised.
\s1 Abraham Receives the Promise
\r (\ref Genesis 15:8–21|GEN 15:8-21\ref*)
\p \v 13 For the promise to Abraham \add and\add* his offspring that he would be heir \add of the\add* world was not \add given\add* through \add the\add* law, but through \add the\add* righteousness that comes by faith. \v 14 For if those \add who live by the\add* law \add are\add* heirs, faith is useless and the promise is worthless, \v 15 because the law brings wrath. And where there is no law, \add there is\add* no transgression.
\p \v 16 Therefore, \add the promise comes\add* by faith, so that \add it\add* may rest on grace \add and\add* may be guaranteed to all \add Abraham’s\add* offspring — not only to those who are of the law, but also to those who are of \add the\add* faith of Abraham. \add He\add* is \add the\add* father of us all. \v 17 As it is written: “I have made you a father of many nations.”\f + \fr 4:17 \ref Genesis 17:5|GEN 17:5\ref*\f* \add He is our father\add* in the presence of God, in whom he believed, the \add God who\add* gives life to the dead and calls \add into\add* being what \add does\add* not yet exist.
\p \v 18 Against all hope, \add Abraham\add* in hope believed and so became \add the\add* father of many nations, just as he had been told, “So \add shall\add* your offspring be.”\f + \fr 4:18 \ref Genesis 15:5|GEN 15:5\ref*\f* \v 19 Without weakening in \add his\add* faith, he acknowledged the decrepitness of his body (since he was about a hundred years old) and the lifelessness of Sarah’s womb. \v 20 Yet he did not waver through disbelief in the promise of God, but was strengthened in his faith \add and\add* gave glory to God, \v 21 being fully persuaded that \add God\add* was able to do what He had promised. \v 22 This is why “it was credited to him as righteousness.”\f + \fr 4:22 \ref Genesis 15:6|GEN 15:6\ref*\f*
\p \v 23 Now \add the words\add* “it was credited to him” were written not only for \add Abraham\add*, \v 24 but also for us, to whom \add righteousness\add* will be credited— for us who believe in Him who raised Jesus our Lord from \add the\add* dead. \v 25 \add He\add* was delivered over to death for our trespasses and was raised to life for our justification.
\c 5
\s1 The Triumph of Faith
\p \v 1 Therefore, since we have been justified through faith, we have\f + \fr 5:1 \ft Or \fqa let us have\f* peace with God through our Lord Jesus Christ, \v 2 through whom we have gained access by faith into this grace in which we stand. And we rejoice\f + \fr 5:2 \ft Or \fqa exult\ft ; also in verses 3 and 11\f* in \add the\add* hope of the glory of God.
\p \v 3 Not only \add that\add*, but we also rejoice in \add our\add* sufferings, because we know that suffering produces perseverance; \v 4 perseverance, character; and character, hope. \v 5 And hope does not disappoint \add us\add*, because God has poured out \add His\add* love into our hearts through \add the\add* Holy Spirit, whom He has given us.
\s1 Christ’s Sacrifice for the Ungodly
\r (\ref John 3:1–21|JHN 3:1-21\ref*)
\p \v 6 For at just \add the\add* right time, while we were still powerless, Christ died for \add the\add* ungodly. \v 7 Very rarely will anyone die for a righteous \add man\add*, though for a good \add man\add* someone \add might\add* possibly dare to die. \v 8 But God proves His love for us in this: While we were still sinners, Christ died for us.
\p \v 9 Therefore, \add since\add* we have now been justified by His blood, how much more shall we be saved from wrath through Him! \v 10 For if, when we were enemies \add of God\add*, we were reconciled to \add Him\add* through the death of His Son, how much more, having been reconciled, shall we be saved through His life! \v 11 Not only that, but we also rejoice in God through our Lord Jesus Christ, through whom we have now received reconciliation.
\s1 Death in Adam, Life in Christ
\r (\ref Genesis 3:1–7|GEN 3:1-7\ref*)
\p \v 12 Therefore, just as sin entered the world through one man, and death through sin, so also death was passed on to all men, because all sinned. \v 13 For sin was in \add the\add* world before \add the\add* law \add was given\add*; but sin is not taken into account when there is no law. \v 14 Nevertheless, death reigned from Adam until Moses, even over those who did not sin in the way that Adam transgressed. He is a pattern of the \add One\add* to come.
\p \v 15 But the gift \add is\add* not like the trespass. For if the many died by the trespass of the one \add man\add*, how much more \add did\add* God’s grace and the gift that came by \add the\add* grace of the one man, Jesus Christ, abound to the many! \v 16 Again, the gift \add is\add* not like \add the result of\add* the one \add man’s\add* sin: The judgment \add that followed\add* one \add sin brought\add* condemnation, but the gift \add that followed\add* many trespasses \add brought\add* justification. \v 17 For if, by the trespass of the one \add man\add*, death reigned through \add that\add* one \add man\add*, how much more \add will\add* those who receive the abundance of grace and of the gift of righteousness reign in life through the one man, Jesus Christ!
\p \v 18 So then, just as one trespass \add brought\add* condemnation for all men, so also one act of righteousness \add brought\add* justification \add and\add* life for all men. \v 19 For just as through the disobedience of the one man the many were made sinners, so also through the obedience of the one \add man\add* the many will be made righteous.
\p \v 20 \add The\add* law came in so that the trespass would increase; but where sin increased, grace increased all the more, \v 21 so that, just as sin reigned in death, so also grace might reign through righteousness to bring eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.
\c 6
\s1 Dead to Sin, Alive to God
\r (\ref 2 Corinthians 4:7–18|2CO 4:7-18\ref*)
\p \v 1 What then shall we say? Shall we continue in sin so that grace may increase? \v 2 Certainly not! How \add can we\add* who died to sin live in it any longer? \v 3 Or aren’t you aware that all of us who were baptized into Christ Jesus were baptized into His death? \v 4 We were therefore buried with Him through baptism into death, in order that, just as Christ was raised from \add the\add* dead through the glory of the Father, we too may walk in newness of life.
\p \v 5 For if we have been united with Him like this in His death, we will certainly also be \add united with Him\add* in His resurrection. \v 6 We know that our old self was crucified with \add Him\add* so that the body of sin might be rendered powerless, \add that\add* we \add should\add* no longer be slaves to sin. \v 7 For \add anyone who\add* has died has been freed from sin.
\p \v 8 Now if we died with Christ, we believe that we will also live with Him. \v 9 \add For\add* we know that \add since\add* Christ was raised from \add the\add* dead, He cannot die again; death no longer has dominion over Him. \v 10 \add The death\add* He died, He died to sin once for all; but \add the life\add* He lives, He lives to God. \v 11 So you too must count yourselves dead to sin, but alive to God in Christ Jesus.
\p \v 12 Therefore do not let sin reign in your mortal body so that you obey its desires. \v 13 \add Do\add* not present the parts of your \add body\add* to sin as instruments of wickedness, but present yourselves to God as \add those who have been brought\add* from death to life; and \add present\add* the parts of your \add body\add* to \add Him\add* as instruments of righteousness. \v 14 For sin shall not be your master, because you are not under law, but under grace.
\s1 The Wages of Sin
\p \v 15 What then? Shall we sin because we are not under law, but under grace? Certainly not! \v 16 Do you not know that \add when\add* you offer yourselves \add as\add* obedient slaves, you are slaves to \add the one\add* you obey, whether \add you are slaves\add* to sin \add leading\add* to death, or to obedience \add leading\add* to righteousness? \v 17 But thanks \add be\add* to God that, though you once were slaves to sin, you wholeheartedly obeyed the form of teaching to which you were committed. \v 18 You have been set free from sin \add and\add* have become slaves to righteousness.
\p \v 19 I am speaking in human terms because of the weakness of your flesh. Just as you used to offer the parts of your \add body\add* in slavery to impurity and to escalating wickedness, so now offer \add them\add* in slavery to righteousness leading to holiness.
\p \v 20 For when you were slaves to sin, you were free of obligation to righteousness. \v 21 What fruit did you reap at that time from the \add things\add* of which you are now ashamed? The outcome of those things \add is\add* death. \v 22 But now \add that\add* you have been set free from sin and have become slaves to God, the fruit you reap \add leads to\add* holiness, and the outcome \add is\add* eternal life. \v 23 For the wages of sin \add is\add* death, but the gift of God \add is\add* eternal life in Christ Jesus our Lord.
\c 7
\s1 Release from the Law
\r (\ref Galatians 3:15–25|GAL 3:15-25\ref*)
\p \v 1 Do you not know, brothers (for I am speaking to those who know \add the\add* law), that the law has authority over a man only as long as he lives? \v 2 For instance, a married woman is bound by law to \add her\add* husband as long as he lives. But if \add her\add* husband dies, she is released from the law of \add marriage\add*. \v 3 So then, if she is \add joined\add* to another man \add while her\add* husband is \add still\add* alive, she is called an adulteress; but if \add her\add* husband dies, she is free from that law \add and\add* is not an adulteress \add if she marries\add* another man.
\p \v 4 Therefore, my brothers, you also died to the law through the body of Christ, that you might belong to another, to \add Him who\add* was raised from \add the\add* dead, in order that we might bear fruit to God. \v 5 For when we \add lived\add* according to the flesh, the sinful passions \add aroused\add* by the law were at work in our bodies, bearing fruit for death. \v 6 But now, having died to what bound us, we have been released from the law, so that we serve in the new way of the Spirit, and not in the old way of the written code.
\s1 God’s Law Is Holy
\p \v 7 What then shall we say? \add Is\add* the law sin? Certainly not! Indeed, I would not have been mindful of sin if not for the law. For I would not have been aware of coveting if the law \add had\add* not said, “Do not covet.”\f + \fr 7:7 \ref Exodus 20:17|EXO 20:17\ref*; Deuteronomy 5:21\f* \v 8 But sin, seizing \add its\add* opportunity through the commandment, produced in me every kind of covetous desire. For apart from \add the\add* law, sin \add is\add* dead.
\p \v 9 Once I was alive apart from \add the\add* law; but \add when\add* the commandment came, sin sprang to life and I died. \v 10 So I discovered that the very commandment that was meant to bring life \add actually brought\add* death. \v 11 For sin, seizing \add its\add* opportunity through the commandment, deceived me and through \add the commandment\add* put \add me\add* to death.
\p \v 12 So then, the law \add is\add* holy, and the commandment \add is\add* holy, righteous, and good.
\s1 Struggling with Sin
\p \v 13 \add Did\add* that which \add is\add* good, then, become death to me? Certainly not! But in order that sin might be exposed as sin, it produced death in me through what \add was\add* good, so that through the commandment sin might become utterly sinful.
\p \v 14 We know that \add the\add* law is spiritual; but I am unspiritual, sold as a slave to sin. \v 15 I do not understand what I do. For what I want \add to do\add*, I do not do. But what I hate, I do. \v 16 And if I do what I do not want \add to do\add*, I admit that the law \add is\add* good. \v 17 In that case, \add it is\add* no longer I who do it, but \add it is\add* sin living in me \add that does it \add*.
\p \v 18 I know that nothing good lives in me, that is, in my flesh; for I have the desire to do what is good, but \add I\add* cannot \add carry it out\add*. \v 19 For I do not do \add the\add* good I want \add to do\add*. Instead, I keep on doing \add the\add* evil I do not want \add to do\add*. \v 20 And if I do what I do not want, \add it is\add* no longer I who do it, but \add it is\add* sin living in me \add that does it \add*.
\p \v 21 So \add this is\add* the principle I have discovered: When I want to do good, evil is right there with me. \v 22 For in \add my\add* inner being I delight in God’s law. \v 23 But I see another law  \add at work \add* in my body, warring against the law of my mind and holding me captive to the law of sin that dwells within \add me\add*.\f + \fr 7:23 \ft Literally \fqa captive to the law of sin being in my members.\f*
\p \v 24 \add What a\add* wretched man I am! Who will rescue me from this body of death? \v 25 Thanks \add be\add* to God, through Jesus Christ our Lord!
\p So then, with \add my\add* mind I serve \add the\add* law of God, but \add with my\add* flesh \add I serve the\add* law of sin.
\c 8
\s1 Walking by the Spirit
\r (\ref Ezekiel 36:16–38|EZK 36:16-38\ref*; \ref Galatians 5:16–26|GAL 5:16-26\ref*)
\p \v 1 Therefore, \add there is\add* now no condemnation for those who \add are\add* in Christ Jesus.\f + \fr 8:1 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa in Christ Jesus, who do not walk according to the flesh but according to the Spirit.\f* \v 2 For in Christ Jesus the law of the Spirit of life \add set\add* you\f + \fr 8:2 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa me\f* free from the law of sin and death. \v 3 For what the law was powerless to do in that it was weakened by the flesh, God \add did\add* by sending His \add own\add* Son in \add the\add* likeness of sinful man, \add as an offering \add* for sin.\f + \fr 8:3 \ft Literally \fqa in the likeness of sinful flesh and for sin\f* He \add thus\add* condemned sin in the flesh, \v 4 so that the righteous standard of the law might be fulfilled in us, who \add do\add* not walk according to \add the\add* flesh but according to \add the\add* Spirit.
\p \v 5 Those who live according to \add the\add* flesh set their minds on the things of the flesh; but those \add who live\add* according to \add the\add* Spirit \add set their minds on\add* the things of the Spirit. \v 6 The mind of the flesh \add is\add* death, but the mind of the Spirit \add is\add* life and peace, \v 7 because the mind of the flesh \add is\add* hostile to God: It does not submit to God’s law, nor can it \add do so\add*. \v 8 Those controlled by \add the\add* flesh\f + \fr 8:8 \ft Literally \fqa Those being in the flesh\ft ; similarly in verse 9\f* cannot please God.
\p \v 9 You, however, are controlled not by \add the\add* flesh, but by \add the\add* Spirit, if \add the\add* Spirit of God lives in you. And if anyone \add does\add* not have \add the\add* Spirit of Christ, he \add does\add* not belong to \add Christ\add*. \v 10 But if Christ \add is\add* in you, \add your\add* body \add is\add* dead because of sin, yet \add your\add* spirit \add is\add* alive\f + \fr 8:10 \ft Or \fqa yet the Spirit is life\f* because of righteousness. \v 11 And if the Spirit of Him who raised Jesus from \add the\add* dead is living in you, He who raised Christ Jesus from \add the\add* dead\f + \fr 8:11 \ft NA, BYZ, and TR \fqa raised Christ from the dead\f* will also give life to your mortal bodies through His Spirit, who lives in you.
\s1 Heirs with Christ
\p \v 12 Therefore, brothers, we have \add an\add* obligation, \add but it is\add* not to the flesh, to live according to \add it\add*. \v 13 For if you live according to \add the\add* flesh, you will die; but if by \add the\add* Spirit you put to death the deeds of the body, you will live. \v 14 For all who are led by \add the\add* Spirit of God are sons of God.
\p \v 15 For you did not receive a spirit of slavery that returns you to fear, but you received \add the\add* Spirit of adoption to sonship, by whom we cry, “Abba! Father!” \v 16 The Spirit Himself testifies with our spirit that we are God’s children. \v 17 And if \add we are\add* children, then \add we are\add* heirs: heirs of God and co-heirs with Christ — if indeed we suffer with \add Him\add*, so that we may also be glorified with \add Him\add*.
\s1 Future Glory
\r (\ref 2 Corinthians 5:1–10|2CO 5:1-10\ref*)
\p \v 18 I consider that \add our\add* present sufferings \add are\add* not comparable to the glory that will be revealed in us. \v 19 The creation waits in eager expectation for the revelation of the sons of God. \v 20 For the creation was subjected to futility, not by its own will, but because of the \add One who\add* subjected \add it\add*, in hope \v 21 that the creation itself will be set free from \add its\add* bondage to decay \add and brought\add* into the glorious freedom of the children of God.
\p \v 22 We know that the whole creation has been groaning together in the pains of childbirth until the present time. \v 23 Not only that, but we ourselves, who have the firstfruits of the Spirit, groan inwardly as we wait eagerly for \add our\add* adoption as sons, the redemption of our bodies. \v 24 For in this hope we were saved; but hope that is seen is no hope \add at all\add*. \add Who\add* hopes for what he can already see? \v 25 But if we hope for what we do not yet see, we wait for \add it\add* patiently.
\p \v 26 In the same way, the Spirit helps us in our weakness. For we do not know how we ought to pray, but the Spirit Himself intercedes \add for us\add* with groans too deep for words. \v 27 And He who searches \add our\add* hearts knows the mind of the Spirit, because \add the Spirit\add* intercedes for \add the\add* saints according to \add the will of\add* God.
\s1 God Works in All Things
\r (\ref Ephesians 1:3–14|EPH 1:3-14\ref*)
\p \v 28 And we know that God works all things together for \add the\add* good of those who love \add Him\add*, who are called according to \add His\add* purpose. \v 29 For those \add God\add* foreknew, He also predestined \add to be\add* conformed to the image of His Son, so that He would be \add the\add* firstborn among many brothers. \v 30 And those He predestined, He also called; \add those\add* He called, He \add also\add* justified; \add those\add* He justified, He also glorified.
\p \v 31 What then shall we say in response to these things? If God \add is\add* for us, who \add can be\add* against us? \v 32 He who did not spare \add His\add* own Son but gave Him up for us all, how \add will He\add* not also, along with Him, freely give us all things? \v 33 Who will bring any charge against God’s elect? \add It is\add* God \add who\add* justifies. \v 34 Who \add is\add* there to condemn \add us\add*? \add For\add* Christ Jesus, who died, and more than that was raised to life, is at \add the\add* right hand of God— and He is interceding for us.
\s1 More than Conquerors
\r (\ref Psalm 44:1–26|PSA 44:1-26\ref*)
\p \v 35 Who shall separate us from the love of Christ? \add Shall\add* trouble or distress or persecution or famine or nakedness or danger or sword? \v 36 As it is written:
\b 
\q1 “For Your sake we face death all day long;
\q2 we are considered as sheep to be slaughtered.”\f + \fr 8:36 \ref Psalm 44:22|PSA 44:22\ref*\f*
\p \v 37 No, in all these things we are more than conquerors through Him who loved us. \v 38 For I am convinced that neither death nor life, neither angels nor principalities, neither the present nor the future, nor any powers, \v 39 neither height nor depth, nor anything else in all creation, will be able to separate us from the love of God that \add is\add* in Christ Jesus our Lord.
\c 9
\s1 Paul’s Concern for the Jews
\p \v 1 I speak \add the\add* truth in Christ; I am not lying, as confirmed by my conscience in \add the\add* Holy Spirit. \v 2 I have deep sorrow and unceasing anguish in my heart. \v 3 For I could wish that I myself were cursed \add and\add* cut off from Christ for the sake of my brothers, my own flesh and blood, \v 4 \add the people of\add* Israel. \add Theirs is\add* the adoption as sons; \add theirs\add* the divine glory and the covenants; \add theirs\add* the giving of the law, the temple worship, and the promises. \v 5 \add Theirs are\add* the patriarchs, and from them \add proceeds  the\add* human \add descent\add* of Christ, who is God over all, forever worthy of praise!\f + \fr 9:5 \ft Or \fqa forever blessed\f* Amen.
\s1 God’s Sovereign Choice
\r (\ref Genesis 25:19–28|GEN 25:19-28\ref*; \ref Malachi 1:1–5|MAL 1:1-5\ref*)
\p \v 6 \add It is\add* not as though God’s word has failed. For not all who \add are\add* descended from Israel \add are\add* Israel. \v 7 Nor because they are Abraham’s descendants \add are\add* they all \add his\add* children. On the contrary, “Through Isaac your offspring will be reckoned.”\f + \fr 9:7 \ref Genesis 21:12|GEN 21:12\ref*\f* \v 8 So it is not the children of the flesh \add who are\add* God’s children, but \add it is\add* the children of the promise who are regarded as offspring. \v 9 For this \add is\add* what \add the\add* promise stated: “At the appointed time I will return, and Sarah will have a son.”\f + \fr 9:9 \ref Genesis 18:14|GEN 18:14\ref*\f*
\p \v 10 Not only that, but Rebecca’s \add children\add* were conceived by one \add man\add*, our father Isaac. \v 11 Yet before \add the twins\add* were born \add or\add* had done anything good or bad, in order that God’s plan of election might stand, \v 12 not by works but by Him who calls, she was told, “The older will serve the younger.”\f + \fr 9:12 \ref Genesis 25:23|GEN 25:23\ref*\f* \v 13 So it is written: “Jacob I loved, but Esau I hated.”\f + \fr 9:13 \ref Malachi 1:2–3|MAL 1:2-3\ref*\f*
\p \v 14 What then shall we say? \add Is\add* God unjust? Certainly not! \v 15 For He says to Moses:
\b 
\q1 “I will have mercy on whom I have mercy,
\q2 and I will have compassion on whom I have compassion.”\f + \fr 9:15 \ref Exodus 33:19|EXO 33:19\ref*\f*
\p \v 16 So then, \add it does\add* not \add depend\add* on man’s desire \add or\add* effort, but on God’s mercy. \v 17 For the Scripture says to Pharaoh: “I raised you up for this very \add purpose\add*, that I might display My power in you, and that My name might be proclaimed in all the earth.”\f + \fr 9:17 \ref Exodus 9:16|EXO 9:16\ref* (see also LXX)\f* \v 18 Therefore \add God\add* has mercy on whom He wants \add to have mercy \add*, and He hardens whom He wants \add to harden \add*.
\p \v 19 One of you will say to me, “Then why does \add God\add* still find fault? For who can resist His will?” \v 20 But who are you, O man, to talk back to God? \add Shall\add* what is formed say to Him who formed \add it\add*, “Why did You make me like this?”\f + \fr 9:20 \ref Isaiah 29:16|ISA 29:16\ref*; Isaiah 45:9\f* \v 21 \add Does\add* not the potter have the right to make from the same lump of clay one vessel for special occasions and \add another\add* for common use?
\p \v 22 What if God, intending to show \add His\add* wrath and make His power \add known\add*, bore with great patience \add the\add* vessels of His wrath, prepared for destruction? \v 23 \add What if He did this\add* to make the riches of His glory \add known\add* to \add the\add* vessels of \add His\add* mercy, whom He prepared in advance for glory — \v 24 including us, whom He has called not only from \add the\add* Jews, but also from \add the\add* Gentiles? \v 25 As He says in Hosea:
\b 
\q1 “I will call \add them\add* ‘My People’ who \add are\add* not My people,
\q2 and \add I will call her\add* ‘\add My\add* Beloved’ who \add is\add* not \add My\add* beloved,”\f + \fr 9:25 \ref Hosea 2:23|HOS 2:23\ref*\f*
\p \v 26 and,
\b 
\q1 “It will happen that in the very place where it was said to them,
\q2 ‘You \add are\add* not My people,’
\q1 they will be called
\q2 ‘sons of \add the\add* living God.’”\f + \fr 9:26 \ref Hosea 1:10|HOS 1:10\ref*\f*
\p \v 27 Isaiah cries out concerning Israel:
\b 
\q1 “Though the number of the Israelites is like the sand of the sea,
\q2 \add only\add* the remnant will be saved.
\q1 \v 28 For \add the\add* Lord will carry out \add His\add* sentence on the earth
\q2 thoroughly and decisively.”\f + \fr 9:28 \ref Isaiah 10:22–23|ISA 10:22-23\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 29 \add It is\add* just as Isaiah foretold:
\b 
\q1 “Unless \add the\add* Lord of Hosts had left us descendants,
\q2 we would have become like Sodom,
\q2 we would have resembled Gomorrah.”\f + \fr 9:29 \ref Isaiah 1:9|ISA 1:9\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\s1 Israel’s Unbelief
\p \v 30 What then will we say? That \add the\add* Gentiles, who did not pursue righteousness, have obtained \add it\add*, a righteousness that \add is\add* by faith; \v 31 but Israel, who pursued a law of righteousness, has not attained \add it\add*. \v 32 Why \add not\add*? Because \add their pursuit was\add* not by faith, but as if \add it were\add* by works. They stumbled over the stumbling stone, \v 33 as it is written:
\b 
\q1 “See, I lay in Zion a stone of stumbling
\q2 and a rock of offense;\f + \fr 9:33 \ref Isaiah 8:14|ISA 8:14\ref*\f*
\q1 and the \add one who\add* believes in Him
\q2 will never be put to shame.”\f + \fr 9:33 \ref Isaiah 28:16|ISA 28:16\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\c 10
\s1 The Word Brings Salvation
\r (\ref Isaiah 65:1–16|ISA 65:1-16\ref*)
\p \v 1 Brothers, my heart’s desire and prayer to God for \add the Israelites is\add* for \add their\add* salvation. \v 2 For I testify about them that they are zealous for God, but not on the basis of knowledge. \v 3 Because they were ignorant of God’s righteousness and sought to establish \add their\add* own, they did not submit to God’s righteousness. \v 4 For Christ \add is the\add* end of the law, to bring righteousness to everyone who believes.
\p \v 5 For concerning the righteousness that is by the law, Moses writes: “The man \add who\add* does these things will live by them.”\f + \fr 10:5 \ref Leviticus 18:5|LEV 18:5\ref*; see also Ezekiel 20:11, 13, and 21.\f* \v 6 But the righteousness that is by faith says: “Do not say in your heart, ‘Who will ascend into heaven?’\f + \fr 10:6 \ref Deuteronomy 30:12|DEU 30:12\ref*\f* (that is, to bring Christ down) \v 7 or, ‘Who will descend into the Abyss?’\f + \fr 10:7 \ft See \ref Deuteronomy 30:13|DEU 30:13\ref*.\f* (that is, to bring Christ up from \add the\add* dead).”
\p \v 8 But what does it say? “The word is near you; \add it is\add* in your mouth and in your heart,”\f + \fr 10:8 \ref Deuteronomy 30:14|DEU 30:14\ref*\f* that is, the word of faith we are proclaiming: \v 9 that if you confess with your mouth, “Jesus \add is\add* Lord,” and believe in your heart that God raised Him from \add the\add* dead, you will be saved. \v 10 For with \add your\add* heart \add you\add* believe \add and are\add* justified, and with \add your\add* mouth \add you\add* confess \add and are\add* saved.
\p \v 11 \add It is\add* just as the Scripture says: “Anyone who believes in Him will never be put to shame.”\f + \fr 10:11 \ref Isaiah 28:16|ISA 28:16\ref* (see also LXX)\f* \v 12 For there is no difference between Jew and Greek: The same Lord \add is Lord\add* of all, \add and\add* gives richly to all who call on Him, \v 13 for, “Everyone who calls on the name of \add the\add* Lord will be saved.”\f + \fr 10:13 \ref Joel 2:32|JOL 2:32\ref*\f*
\p \v 14 How then can they call on \add the One\add* in whom they have not believed? And how can they believe in \add the One\add* of whom they have not heard? And how can they hear without \add someone\add* to preach? \v 15 And how can they preach unless they are sent? As it is written: “How beautiful \add are\add* the feet of those who bring good news!”\f + \fr 10:15 \ft Literally \fqa good news of good things\ft ; BYZ and TR \fqa How beautiful are the feet of those who bring good news of peace, who bring good news of good things\ft ; \ref Isaiah 52:7|ISA 52:7\ref*\f*
\p \v 16 But not all of them welcomed the good news. For Isaiah says, “Lord, who has believed our message?”\f + \fr 10:16 \ref Isaiah 53:1|ISA 53:1\ref*\f* \v 17 Consequently, faith \add comes\add* by hearing, and hearing by \add the\add* word of Christ.
\p \v 18 But I ask, did they not hear? Indeed \add they did\add*:
\b 
\q1 “Their voice has gone out into all the earth,
\q2 their words to the ends of the world.”\f + \fr 10:18 \ref Psalm 19:4|PSA 19:4\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 19 I ask instead, \add did\add* Israel not understand? First, Moses says:
\b 
\q1 “I will make you jealous by \add those who are\add* not a nation;
\q2 I will make you angry by a nation without understanding.”\f + \fr 10:19 \ref Deuteronomy 32:21|DEU 32:21\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 20 And Isaiah boldly says:
\b 
\q1 “I was found by those who did not seek Me;
\q2 I revealed Myself to those who did not ask for Me.”\f + \fr 10:20 \ref Isaiah 65:1|ISA 65:1\ref*\f*
\p \v 21 But as for Israel he says:
\b 
\q1 “All day long I have held out My hands
\q2 to \add a\add* disobedient and obstinate people.”\f + \fr 10:21 \ref Isaiah 65:2|ISA 65:2\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\c 11
\s1 A Remnant Chosen by Grace
\p \v 1 I ask then, \add did\add* God reject His people? Certainly not! I am an Israelite \add myself\add*, a descendant of Abraham, from \add the\add* tribe of Benjamin. \v 2 God did not reject His people, whom He foreknew. Do you not know what the Scripture says about Elijah, how he appealed to God against Israel: \v 3 “Lord, they have killed Your prophets \add and\add* torn down Your altars. I \add am\add* the only one left, and they are seeking my life \add as well \add*”\f + \fr 11:3 \ref 1 Kings 19:10|1KI 19:10\ref*, 14\f*?
\p \v 4 \add And\add* what \add was\add* the divine reply to him? “I have reserved for Myself seven thousand men who \add have\add* not bowed \add the\add* knee to Baal.”\f + \fr 11:4 \ref 1 Kings 19:18|1KI 19:18\ref*\f*
\p \v 5 In the same way, at the present time there is a remnant chosen by grace. \v 6 And if \add it is\add* by grace, \add then it is\add* no longer by works. Otherwise, grace \add would\add* no longer be grace.\f + \fr 11:6 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa But if it is by works, then it is no longer grace; otherwise work is no longer work.\f*
\p \v 7 What then? What Israel was seeking, it failed to obtain, but the elect \add did\add*. The others were hardened, \v 8 as it is written:
\b 
\q1 “God gave them a spirit of stupor,
\q2 eyes that could not see,
\q2 and ears that could not hear,
\q1 to this very day.”\f + \fr 11:8 \ft See \ref Deuteronomy 29:4|DEU 29:4\ref* and Isaiah 29:10.\f*
\p \v 9 And David says:
\b 
\q1 “\add May\add* their table become a snare and a trap,
\q2 a stumbling block and a retribution to them.
\q1 \v 10 \add May\add* their eyes be darkened \add so\add* they cannot see,
\q2 and their backs be bent forever.”\f + \fr 11:10 \ref Psalm 69:22–23|PSA 69:22-23\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\s1 The Ingrafting of the Gentiles
\p \v 11 I ask then, did they stumble so as to fall beyond recovery?\f + \fr 11:11 \ft Or \fqa did they stumble so as to lose their share? \ft Literally \fqa did they stumble that they might fall?\f* Certainly not! However, because of their trespass, salvation \add has come\add* to the Gentiles to make \add Israel\add* jealous. \v 12 But if their trespass \add means\add* riches for \add the\add* world, and their failure \add means\add* riches for \add the\add* Gentiles, how much greater \add riches  will\add* their fullness \add bring\add*!
\p \v 13 I am speaking to you Gentiles. Inasmuch \add as\add* I am \add the\add* apostle to \add the\add* Gentiles, I magnify my ministry \v 14 in the hope that I may provoke my \add own\add* people \add to jealousy\add* and save some of them. \v 15 For if their rejection \add is the\add* reconciliation of \add the\add* world, what \add will their\add* acceptance \add be\add* but life from \add the\add* dead? \v 16 If the first part \add of the dough is\add* holy, so \add is\add* the whole batch; if the root \add is\add* holy, so \add are\add* the branches.
\p \v 17 Now if some branches have been broken off, and you, a wild olive shoot, have been grafted in among \add the others\add* to share in the nourishment of the olive root, \v 18 \add do\add* not boast over those branches. If \add you do, remember this\add*: You \add do\add* not support the root, but the root \add supports \add* you.
\p \v 19 You will say then, “Branches were broken off so that I could be grafted in.” \v 20 That is correct: They were broken off because of unbelief, but you stand by faith. \add Do\add* not be arrogant, but be afraid. \v 21 For if God \add did\add* not spare the natural branches, \add He will\add* certainly not\f + \fr 11:21 \ft Or \fqa He will perhaps not\ft ; SBL, NE, and WH \fqa He will not\f* spare you \add either\add*.
\p \v 22 Take notice, therefore, \add of the\add* kindness and severity of God: severity to those who fell, but kindness to you, if you continue in \add His\add* kindness. Otherwise you also will be cut off. \v 23 And if they do not persist in unbelief, they will be grafted in, for God is able to graft them \add in\add* again. \v 24 For if you were cut from a wild olive tree, and contrary to nature were grafted into \add one that is\add* cultivated, how much more \add readily  will\add* these, the natural \add branches\add*, be grafted into \add their\add* own olive tree!
\s1 All Israel Will Be Saved
\p \v 25 I do not want you to be ignorant of this mystery, brothers, so that you will not be conceited: A hardening in part has come to Israel, until the full number of the Gentiles has come in. \v 26 And so all Israel will be saved, as it is written:
\b 
\q1 “The Deliverer will come from Zion;
\q2 He will remove godlessness from Jacob.
\q1 \v 27 And this \add is\add* My covenant with them
\q2 when I take away their sins.”\f + \fr 11:27 \ref Isaiah 27:9|ISA 27:9\ref* and Isaiah 59:20–21; see also LXX\f*
\p \v 28 Regarding the gospel, \add they are\add* enemies on your account; but regarding election, \add they are\add* loved on account of the patriarchs. \v 29 For God’s gifts and \add His\add* call \add are\add* irrevocable.
\p \v 30 Just as you \add who\add* formerly disobeyed God have now received mercy through their disobedience, \v 31 so they too have now disobeyed, in order that they too \add may\add* now receive mercy through the mercy \add shown to\add* you.\f + \fr 11:31 \ft Literally \fqa may now receive mercy through your mercy\ft ; BYZ and TR \fqa may receive mercy through your mercy\f* \v 32 For God has consigned everyone to disobedience so that He may have mercy on everyone.
\s1 A Hymn of Praise
\r (\ref Isaiah 40:9–31|ISA 40:9-31\ref*)
\q1 \v 33 O, \add the\add* depth of \add the\add* riches
\q2 of \add the\add* wisdom and knowledge of God!
\q1 How unsearchable \add are\add* His judgments,
\q2 and untraceable His ways!
\q1 \v 34 “Who has known \add the\add* mind of \add the\add* Lord?
\q2 Or who has been His counselor?”\f + \fr 11:34 \ref Isaiah 40:13|ISA 40:13\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\q1 \v 35 “Who has first given to God,
\q2 that God should repay him?”\f + \fr 11:35 \ref Job 41:11|JOB 41:11\ref*\f*
\q1 \v 36 For from Him and through Him and to Him \add are\add* all things.
\q2 To Him \add be\add* the glory forever! Amen.
\c 12
\s1 Living Sacrifices
\r (\ref 1 Corinthians 3:16–23|1CO 3:16-23\ref*; \ref 1 Corinthians 6:18–20|1CO 6:18-20\ref*)
\p \v 1 Therefore I urge you, brothers, on account of God’s mercy, to offer your bodies \add as\add* living sacrifices, holy \add and\add* pleasing to God, which is your spiritual service of worship.\f + \fr 12:1 \ft Or \fqa your reasonable service\f* \v 2 Do not be conformed to this world, but be transformed by the renewing of \add your\add* mind. Then you will be able to test and approve what \add is\add* the good, pleasing, and perfect will of God.
\p \v 3 For by the grace given me I say to every one of you: Do not think of yourself more highly than you ought, but think of yourself with sober judgment, according to \add the\add* measure of faith God has given \add you\add*. \v 4 Just as each of us has one body with many members, and not all members have the same function, \v 5 so in Christ we who are many \add are\add* one body, and each member \add belongs to\add* one another.
\p \v 6 We have different gifts according to the grace given us. If \add one’s gift is\add* prophecy, \add let him use\add* it in proportion to \add his\add* faith; \v 7 \add if it is\add* serving, let him serve; \add if it is\add* teaching, let him teach; \v 8 \add if it is\add* encouraging, let him encourage; \add if it is\add* giving, let him give generously; \add if it is\add* leading, \add let him lead\add* with diligence; \add if it is\add* showing mercy, \add let him do it\add* cheerfully.
\s1 Love, Zeal, Hope, Hospitality
\r (\ref John 13:31–35|JHN 13:31-35\ref*; \ref 1 John 3:11–24|1JN 3:11-24\ref*)
\p \v 9 Love must be sincere. Detest what \add is\add* evil; cling to what \add is\add* good. \v 10 Be devoted to one another in brotherly love. Outdo yourselves in honoring one another.
\p \v 11 Do not let \add your\add* zeal subside; keep your spiritual fervor, serving the Lord.
\p \v 12 Be joyful in hope, patient in affliction, persistent in prayer.
\p \v 13 Share with the saints who \add are\add* in need. Practice hospitality.
\s1 Forgiveness
\r (\ref Matthew 18:21–35|MAT 18:21-35\ref*)
\p \v 14 Bless those who persecute you. Bless and \add do\add* not curse. \v 15 Rejoice with those who rejoice; weep with those who weep. \v 16 Live in harmony with one another. Do not be proud, but associate with the lowly. \add Do\add* not be conceited.
\p \v 17 Do not repay anyone evil for evil. Carefully consider what is right in the eyes of everybody. \v 18 If \add it is\add* possible on your \add part\add*, live at peace with everyone.
\p \v 19 \add Do\add* not avenge yourselves, beloved, but leave room for \add God’s\add* wrath. For it is written: “Vengeance \add is\add* Mine; I will repay, says \add the\add* Lord.”\f + \fr 12:19 \ref Deuteronomy 32:35|DEU 32:35\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 20 On the contrary,
\b 
\q1 “If your enemy is hungry, feed him;
\q2 if he is thirsty, give him a drink.
\q1 For in so doing,
\q2 you will heap burning coals on his head.”\f + \fr 12:20 \ref Proverbs 25:21–22|PRO 25:21-22\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 21 \add Do\add* not be overcome by evil, but overcome evil with good.
\c 13
\s1 Submission to Authorities
\r (\ref 1 Peter 2:13–20|1PE 2:13-20\ref*)
\p \v 1 Everyone must submit himself to the governing authorities, for there is no authority except \add that which is from\add* God. \add The authorities\add* that exist have been appointed by God. \v 2 Consequently, whoever resists authority is opposing what God has set in place, and those who \add do so\add* will bring judgment on themselves.
\p \v 3 For rulers are not a terror to good conduct, but to bad. Do you want to be unafraid of the one in authority? \add Then\add* do what \add is\add* right, and you will have his approval. \v 4 For he is God’s servant for your good. But if you do wrong, be afraid, for he does not carry the sword in vain. He is God’s servant, an agent of retribution to the wrongdoer.
\p \v 5 Therefore \add it is\add* necessary to submit \add to authority \add*, not only to avoid punishment, but also as a matter of conscience. \v 6 This is also why you pay taxes. For \add the authorities\add* are God’s servants, who devote themselves to \add their work\add*. \v 7 Pay everyone what you owe \add him\add*: taxes to whom taxes \add are due\add*, revenue to whom revenue \add is due\add*, respect to whom respect \add is due\add*, honor to whom honor \add is due\add*.
\s1 Love Fulfills the Law
\r (\ref Leviticus 19:9–18|LEV 19:9-18\ref*)
\p \v 8 Be indebted to no one, except to one another \add in\add* love. For he who loves \add his\add* neighbor has fulfilled \add the\add* law. \v 9 The \add commandments\add* “Do not commit adultery,” “Do not murder,” “Do not steal,” “Do not covet,”\f + \fr 13:9 \ref Exodus 20:13–17|EXO 20:13-17\ref*; Deuteronomy 5:17–21\f* and any other commandments, are summed up in this \add one\add* decree: “Love your neighbor as yourself.”\f + \fr 13:9 \ref Leviticus 19:18|LEV 19:18\ref*\f* \v 10 Love does no wrong to \add its\add* neighbor. Therefore love \add is the\add* fulfillment of \add the\add* law.
\s1 The Day Is Near
\p \v 11 And \add do\add* this, understanding the occasion. \add The\add* hour has come for you to wake up from \add your\add* slumber, for our salvation \add is\add* nearer now than when we \add first\add* believed. \v 12 The night is nearly over; the day has drawn near. So let us lay aside the deeds of darkness and put on the armor of light. \v 13 Let us behave decently, as in \add the\add* daytime, not in carousing and drunkenness, not in sexual immorality and debauchery, not in dissension and jealousy. \v 14 Instead, clothe yourselves with\f + \fr 13:14 \ft Or \fqa put on\ft ; see verse 12.\f* the Lord Jesus Christ, and make no provision for \add the\add* desires of the flesh.
\c 14
\s1 The Law of Liberty
\r (\ref Matthew 7:1–6|MAT 7:1-6\ref*; \ref Luke 6:37–42|LUK 6:37-42\ref*)
\p \v 1 Accept him whose faith is weak, without passing judgment on \add his\add* opinions.\f + \fr 14:1 \ft Or \fqa without quarreling over disputable matters\f* \v 2 For one \add person\add* has faith to eat all things, while another, who is weak, eats \add only\add* vegetables. \v 3 The \add one who\add* eats \add everything\add* must not belittle the \add one who does not\add*, and the \add one who\add* does not eat \add everything\add* must not judge the \add one who does\add*, for God has accepted him. \v 4 Who are you to judge someone else’s servant? To \add his\add* own master he stands or falls. And he will stand, for the Lord is able to make him stand.
\p \v 5 One \add person\add* regards \add a certain\add* day above \add the others\add*, while someone \add else\add* considers every day \add alike\add*. Each \add one\add* should be fully convinced in \add his\add* own mind. \v 6 He who observes \add a special\add* day \add does so\add* to \add the\add* Lord;\f + \fr 14:6 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa he who does not regard the day, to the Lord he does not regard it;\f* he who eats \add does so\add* to \add the\add* Lord, for he gives thanks to God; and he who abstains \add does so\add* to \add the\add* Lord and gives thanks to God.
\p \v 7 For none of us lives to himself \add alone\add*, and none \add of us \add* dies to himself \add alone\add*. \v 8 If we live, we live to the Lord, and if we die, we die to the Lord. So whether we live \add or\add* die, we belong to the Lord. \v 9 For this reason Christ died and returned to life, that He might be the Lord of both \add the\add* dead and \add the\add* living.
\p \v 10 Why, then, \add do\add* you judge your brother? Or why \add do\add* you belittle your brother? For we will all stand before God’s judgment seat. \v 11 It is written:
\b 
\q1 “\add As surely as\add* I live,
\q2 says \add the\add* Lord,
\q1 every knee will bow before Me;
\q2 every tongue will confess to God.”\f + \fr 14:11 \ft Or \fqa will give praise to God \ft or \fqa will acknowledge God\ft ; \ref Isaiah 45:23|ISA 45:23\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 12 So then, each of us will give an account of himself to God.
\s1 The Law of Love
\r (\ref Ezekiel 14:1–11|EZK 14:1-11\ref*; \ref 1 Corinthians 8:1–13|1CO 8:1-13\ref*)
\p \v 13 Therefore let us stop judging one another. Instead, make up your mind not to put \add any\add* stumbling block or obstacle in your brother’s way.
\p \v 14 I am convinced and fully persuaded in \add the\add* Lord Jesus that nothing \add is\add* unclean in itself. But if \add anyone\add* regards something as unclean, \add then\add* for him \add it is\add* unclean. \v 15 If your brother is distressed by \add what you eat\add*, you are no longer acting in love. \add Do\add* not by \add your eating\add* destroy your \add brother\add*, for whom Christ died.
\p \v 16 \add Do\add* not \add allow\add* what you \add consider\add* good, then, to be spoken of as evil. \v 17 For the kingdom of God is not \add a matter of\add* eating and drinking, but of righteousness, peace, and joy in \add the\add* Holy Spirit. \v 18 For whoever serves Christ in this way \add is\add* pleasing to God and approved by men.
\p \v 19 So then, let us pursue what leads to peace and to mutual edification. \v 20 \add Do\add* not destroy the work of God for the sake of food. All \add food is\add* clean, but \add it is\add* wrong for a man to let his eating be a stumbling block. \v 21 \add It is\add* better not to eat meat \add or\add* drink wine \add or to do anything to cause\add* your brother to stumble.\f + \fr 14:21 \ft SBL, BYZ, and TR include \fqa or to be hindered or weakened\ft .\f*
\p \v 22 Keep your belief \add about such matters\add* between yourself \add and\add* God.\f + \fr 14:22 \ft Literally \fqa Keep the faith that you have to yourself before God\f* Blessed \add is\add* the \add one who\add* does not condemn himself by what he approves. \v 23 But the \add one who\add* has doubts is condemned if he eats, because \add his eating is\add* not from faith; and everything that \add is\add* not from faith is sin.\f + \fr 14:23 \ft Some manuscripts place the text of \ref Romans 16:25–27|ROM 16:25-27\ref* here.\f*
\c 15
\s1 Accept One Another
\p \v 1 We who \add are\add* strong ought to bear with the shortcomings of the weak and not to please ourselves. \v 2 Each of us should please \add his\add* neighbor for \add his\add* good, to build \add him\add* up. \v 3 For even Christ \add did\add* not please Himself, but as it is written: “The insults of those who insult You have fallen on Me.”\f + \fr 15:3 \ref Psalm 69:9|PSA 69:9\ref*\f* \v 4 For everything that was written in the past was written for our instruction, so that through endurance and the encouragement of the Scriptures, we might have hope.
\p \v 5 Now \add may\add* the God \add who gives\add* endurance and encouragement grant you harmony with one another in Christ Jesus, \v 6 so that with one mind \add and\add* one voice you may glorify the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ.
\s1 Christ the Servant of Jews and Gentiles
\p \v 7 Accept one another, then, just as Christ accepted you, in order to bring glory to God. \v 8 For I tell \add you that\add* Christ has become a servant of \add the\add* circumcised on behalf of God’s truth, to confirm the promises made to the patriarchs, \v 9 \add so that\add* the Gentiles may glorify God for \add His\add* mercy. As it is written:
\b 
\q1 “Therefore I will praise You among \add the\add* Gentiles;
\q2 I will sing hymns to Your name.”\f + \fr 15:9 \ref 2 Samuel 22:50|2SA 22:50\ref*; Psalm 18:49\f*
\p \v 10 Again, it says:
\b 
\q1 “Rejoice, O Gentiles, with His people.”\f + \fr 15:10 \ref Deuteronomy 32:43|DEU 32:43\ref*\f*
\p \v 11 And again:
\b 
\q1 “Praise the Lord, all \add you\add* Gentiles,
\q2 and extol Him, all \add you\add* peoples.”\f + \fr 15:11 \ref Psalm 117:1|PSA 117:1\ref*\f*
\p \v 12 And once more, Isaiah says:
\b 
\q1 “The Root of Jesse will appear,
\q2 \add One who\add* will arise to rule over \add the\add* Gentiles;
\q2 in Him \add the\add* Gentiles will put their hope.”\f + \fr 15:12 \ref Isaiah 11:10|ISA 11:10\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 13 Now \add may\add* the God of hope fill you with all joy and peace as you believe \add in Him\add*, so that you may overflow with hope by \add the\add* power \add of the\add* Holy Spirit.
\s1 Paul the Minister to the Gentiles
\p \v 14 I myself am convinced, my brothers, that you yourselves are full of goodness, brimming with knowledge, and able to instruct one another. \v 15 However, I have written you a bold reminder on some points, because of the grace God has given me \v 16 to be a minister of Christ Jesus to the Gentiles in the priestly service of the gospel of God, so that the Gentiles might become \add an\add* offering acceptable \add to God\add*, sanctified by \add the\add* Holy Spirit.
\p \v 17 Therefore I exult in Christ Jesus in  \add my service \add* to God. \v 18 I will not presume to speak of anything except what Christ has accomplished through me in leading \add the\add* Gentiles to obedience by word and deed, \v 19 by \add the\add* power of signs and wonders, \add and\add* by \add the\add* power of \add the\add* Spirit of God.\f + \fr 15:19 \ft SBL \fqa the power of the Spirit\ft ; NE and WH \fqa the power of the Holy Spirit\f* So from Jerusalem all the way around to Illyricum, I have fully proclaimed the gospel of Christ.
\p \v 20 In this way I have aspired to preach the gospel where Christ was not known, so that I would not be building on someone \add else’s\add* foundation. \v 21 Rather, as it is written:
\b 
\q1 “Those who were not told about Him will see,
\q2 and those who have not heard will understand.”\f + \fr 15:21 \ref Isaiah 52:15|ISA 52:15\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 22 That is why I have often been hindered from coming to you.
\s1 Paul’s Travel Plans
\r (\ref 1 Corinthians 16:5–9|1CO 16:5-9\ref*)
\p \v 23 But now that \add there are\add* no further opportunities for me in these regions, and since I have longed for many years to visit you, \v 24 I hope to see you on my way to Spain. And \add after\add* I have enjoyed your company for a while, you can equip me \add for my journey\add*.
\p \v 25 Now, however, I am on my way to Jerusalem to serve the saints \add there\add*. \v 26 For Macedonia and Achaia were pleased to make a contribution for the poor among the saints in Jerusalem. \v 27 They were pleased \add to do it\add*, and indeed they owe it to them. For if the Gentiles have shared in their spiritual \add blessings\add*, they are obligated to minister to them with material \add blessings\add*.
\p \v 28 So after I have completed this \add service\add* and have safely delivered this bounty to them, I will set off to Spain by way of you. \v 29 I know that when I come to you, I will come in \add the\add* fullness of the blessing\f + \fr 15:29 \ft TR and BYZ include \fqa of the gospel\ft .\f* of Christ.
\p \v 30 Now I urge you, brothers, by our Lord Jesus Christ and by the love of the Spirit, to join me in my struggle by praying to God for me. \v 31 \add Pray\add* that I may be delivered from the unbelievers in Judea, and \add that\add* my service in Jerusalem may be acceptable to the saints \add there\add*, \v 32 so that by God’s will I may come to you with joy and together with you be refreshed.
\p \v 33 The God of peace \add be\add* with all of you. Amen.\f + \fr 15:33 \ft Some manuscripts do not include \fqa Amen.\ft One early manuscript places the text of \ref Romans 16:25–27|ROM 16:25-27\ref* here.\f*
\c 16
\s1 Personal Greetings and Love
\p \v 1 I commend to you our sister Phoebe, a servant\f + \fr 16:1 \ft Or \fqa deaconess\f* of the church in Cenchrea. \v 2 Welcome her in \add the\add* Lord in a manner worthy of \add the\add* saints, and assist her with anything she may need from you. For she has been a great help to many \add people\add*, including me.
\p \v 3 Greet Prisca\f + \fr 16:3 \fqa Prisca \ft is a variant of \fqa Priscilla\ft ; see \ref Acts 18:2|ACT 18:2\ref*.\f* and Aquila, my fellow workers in Christ Jesus, \v 4 who have risked their \add lives\add* for me. Not only I but all the churches of the Gentiles are grateful \add to them\add*. \v 5 \add Greet also\add* the church \add that meets\add* at their house.
\p Greet my beloved Epenetus, who was the first \add convert\add* to Christ in \add the province of\add* Asia.\f + \fr 16:5 \ft Literally \fqa in Asia\ft ; Asia was a Roman province in what is now western Turkey. \f*
\p \v 6 Greet Mary, who has worked very hard for you.
\p \v 7 Greet Andronicus and Junia,\f + \fr 16:7 \ft Some translators \fqa Junias\f* my fellow countrymen and fellow prisoners. \add They\add* are outstanding among the apostles, and \add they\add* were in Christ before I \add was\add*.
\p \v 8 Greet Ampliatus, my beloved in \add the\add* Lord.
\p \v 9 Greet Urbanus, our fellow worker in Christ, and my beloved Stachys.
\p \v 10 Greet Apelles, who \add is\add* approved in Christ.
\p Greet those who belong to the \add household\add* of Aristobulus.
\p \v 11 Greet Herodion, my fellow countryman.
\p Greet those from the \add household\add* of Narcissus who are in \add the\add* Lord.
\p \v 12 Greet Tryphena and Tryphosa,  \add women \add* who have worked hard in \add the\add* Lord.
\p Greet \add my\add* beloved Persis, who has worked very hard in \add the\add* Lord.
\p \v 13 Greet Rufus, chosen in \add the\add* Lord, and his mother, \add who has been a mother\add* to me as well.
\p \v 14 Greet Asyncritus, Phlegon, Hermes, Patrobas, Hermas, and the brothers with them.
\p \v 15 Greet Philologus and Julia, Nereus and his sister, and Olympas and all the saints with them.
\p \v 16 Greet one another with a holy kiss.
\p All the churches of Christ send you greetings.
\s1 Avoid Divisions
\r (\ref Titus 3:9–11|TIT 3:9-11\ref*)
\p \v 17 Now I urge you, brothers, to watch out for those who create divisions and obstacles \add that are\add* contrary to the teaching you have learned. Turn away from them. \v 18 For such people \add are\add* not serving our Lord Christ, but their \add own\add* appetites. By smooth talk and flattery they deceive the hearts of the naive.
\p \v 19 Everyone \add has heard\add* about your obedience, so I rejoice over you. But I want you to be wise about what \add is\add* good and innocent about what \add is\add* evil.
\p \v 20 The God of peace will soon crush Satan under your feet. The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ\f + \fr 16:20 \ft NA, NE, and WH \fqa Lord Jesus\f* \add be\add* with you.
\s1 Greetings from Paul’s Fellow Workers
\r (\ref Colossians 4:7–14|COL 4:7-14\ref*)
\p \v 21 Timothy, my fellow worker, sends you greetings, as do Lucius, Jason, and Sosipater, my fellow countrymen.
\p \v 22 I, Tertius, who wrote down this letter, greet you in \add the\add* Lord.
\p \v 23 Gaius, who has hosted me and all the church, sends you greetings.
\p Erastus, the city treasurer, sends you greetings, \add as does our\add* brother Quartus.\f + \fr 16:23 \ft SBL, BYZ, and TR include \fqa \fv 24\fv*May the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all. Amen.\f*
\s1 Doxology
\r (\ref Jude 1:24–25|JUD 24-25\ref*)
\p \v 25 Now to Him who is able to strengthen you by my gospel and by the proclamation of Jesus Christ, according to \add the\add* revelation of \add the\add* mystery concealed for ages past \v 26 but now revealed \add and\add* made known through the writings of the prophets by \add the\add* command of the eternal God, in order to lead all nations to the obedience \add that comes from\add* faith\f + \fr 16:26 \ft Literally \fqa the obedience of faith\f* — \v 27 \add to the\add* only wise God \add be\add* glory forever through Jesus Christ! Amen.\f + \fr 16:27 \ft Some manuscripts place the text of verses 25–27 after \ref Romans 14:23|ROM 14:23\ref* or after Romans 15:33.\f*
